<article class="post lp-issue">
<header class="post-header">
<p class="lp-folio"><span class="lp-folio-number">No. 12</span><time datetime="2026-10-12">12 October 2026</time><span>1 min read</span></p>
<p class="post-kind"><a href="/blog/category/content-strategy/">Content strategy</a></p>
<h1>Planning a topic cluster in an afternoon</h1>
<p class="dek">A topic cluster is one main post and several shorter posts that link to it. How to plan one with a sheet of paper.</p>
<div class="post-meta">
<address>By <a rel="author" href="/blog/authors/asif/">Asif</a></address>
</div>
</header>
<section class="lp-inside" aria-labelledby="lp-inside">
<h2 id="lp-inside">In this issue</h2>
<ol class="lp-inside-list" role="list">
<li><span class="lp-inside-part">Feature</span><span class="lp-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="lp-inside-title">A cluster in an afternoon</span></li>
<li><span class="lp-inside-part">The tool</span><span class="lp-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="lp-inside-title">A sheet of paper</span></li>
<li><span class="lp-inside-part">Letters</span><span class="lp-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="lp-inside-title">How many posts is enough</span></li>
</ol>
</section>

<div class="post-body">
<p>A topic cluster is a group of posts on one subject: one main post that covers the whole subject, and several shorter posts that each answer one question in depth. They all link to each other. Together they show search engines that your site knows the subject, which is what <a href="/blog/glossary/topical-authority/">Topical authority</a> means.</p>
<h2 id="plan-it-on-one-page">Plan it on one page <a class="heading-link" href="#plan-it-on-one-page" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick the subject your customers ask about most.</strong> For a bakery that sells online, that might be &quot;sourdough at home&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Write the main question at the top:</strong> &quot;How do I bake sourdough at home?&quot; That becomes the main post.</li>
<li><strong>List the smaller questions underneath:</strong> how to feed a starter, why the dough is sticky, which flour to buy. Each becomes a short post.</li>
<li><strong>Draw the links.</strong> Every short post links to the main post, and the main post links to every short post.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="write-in-the-right-order">Write in the right order <a class="heading-link" href="#write-in-the-right-order" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Write the short posts first. They are quicker, and they teach you what the main post needs to say. Then write the main post and add the links. <a href="/blog/why-internal-links-matter/">Why internal links matter</a> has the rules for the anchor text.</p>

</div>
<p class="lp-end"><span class="lp-end-mark" aria-hidden="true">&#10086;</span> End of the issue <span class="lp-end-number">No. 12</span></p>
